  AssetFactory — Phase 3
    deployment
      √ deploys with correct admin address and an empty registry (101ms)
      √ rejects a zero admin
    issuer authorisation
      √ admin can authorise an issuer
      √ authorising an already-authorised issuer or the zero address reverts
      √ admin can revoke an issuer
      √ revoking an address that is not authorised reverts
      √ non-admin cannot authorise or revoke an issuer
    asset creation
      √ authorised issuer can create an asset (deploys AssetToken + AssetSale)
      √ created asset is recorded in the registry with full metadata and tokenToSale (38ms)
      √ issuer creates two different assets that are registered independently
      √ unauthorised address, including the admin, cannot create an asset
      √ createAsset with unitPriceWei == 0 reverts
      √ createAsset with totalSupply == 0 reverts
      √ getAsset reverts for an ID that was never assigned (38ms)
      √ rejects an out-of-range assetType
    revocation scope
      √ revoked issuer cannot create new assets but existing contracts keep operating (58ms)
    issuer isolation
      √ issuer A cannot whitelist investors on issuer B's token
      √ issuer A cannot allocate, withdraw, close or recover on issuer B's sale

  AssetSale — Phase 3
    deployment
      √ deploys with correct assetToken, pricePerToken, and issuer
      √ rejects a zero token, zero issuer, or zero price
      √ exposes no function to change the price after creation
    allocation
      √ issuer can allocate tokens (whitelist sale, approve, allocate)
      √ allocate reverts until the issuer whitelists the sale contract
      √ allocate without an approval reverts
      √ non-issuer cannot allocate (45ms)
      √ allocate with insufficient issuer balance reverts
      √ allocate of zero reverts
    buying (instant subscription flow)
      √ whitelisted investor buys 100 tokens with exact ETH payment
      √ investor underpays: reverts
      √ investor overpays: reverts
      √ unapproved investor attempts purchase: reverts
      √ revoked investor cannot buy
      √ investor buys more than inventory: reverts
      √ buying the entire inventory succeeds and leaves nothing for sale
      √ buy of zero quantity reverts
      √ rejects plain ETH sent outside buyTokens
    proceeds
      √ issuer can withdraw ETH proceeds after a purchase (43ms)
      √ non-issuer withdraws proceeds: reverts
      √ withdrawProceeds with zero balance reverts
      √ invariant 8: a re-entrant contract issuer is paid exactly once and the balance is zero (49ms)
      √ a failed payout reverts and leaves the proceeds claimable
    closing and inventory recovery
      √ issuer can close the sale, after which buying and allocating revert (65ms)
      √ recoverUnsoldTokens reverts while the sale is open
      √ issuer recovers all unsold inventory after closing and can still withdraw proceeds
      √ non-issuer cannot close the sale or recover inventory
    invariants
      √ invariant 3: sale ETH balance equals tokens sold times pricePerToken minus withdrawals (43ms)
      √ invariant 2: supply equals the sum of balances, and holders are eligible absent revocation (96ms)
    Phase 3 lifecycle demonstration
      √ create 10,000 CPT, whitelist sale, deposit 5,000, investor pays 0.1 ETH for 100 CPT

  AssetToken — D5 pause and forced transfer (spec-v1 §5 D5 functions)
    pause / unpause
      √ the issuer pauses and unpauses, emitting OpenZeppelin's Paused and Unpaused (89ms)
      √ rejects pause and unpause from an account without DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE
      √ rejects pause from a compliance-only delegate
      √ lets a delegated DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE holder pause, because authority is the role
      √ removes pause and forcedTransfer from a delegated admin whose role is revoked (111ms)
      √ removes pause and forcedTransfer from a delegated admin whose role is renounced (39ms)
      √ rejects pausing twice and unpausing when not paused
      √ does not let one issuer pause another issuer's token (invariant 7)
    pause invariant (restated invariant 5)
      √ blocks transfer and transferFrom while paused and restores them after unpause
      √ blocks AssetSale allocate, buyTokens and recoverUnsoldTokens while paused
      √ still allows whitelist changes and ETH withdrawal while paused
    forcedTransfer
      √ moves a revoked holder's balance to a whitelisted address with Transfer and ForcedTransfer
      √ works while paused
      √ rejects callers without DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE, including a compliance delegate
      √ rejects taking from a whitelisted holder: a live investor or the sale contract
      √ rejects the zero address as source, so it cannot mint
      √ rejects a recipient that is not whitelisted, including the zero address
      √ rejects an amount above the holder's balance
      √ does not let one issuer force-transfer on another issuer's token (invariant 7)
      √ recovers a lost-key holding: revoke, then force-transfer to a new wallet
    forced-transfer invariant (restated invariant 6)
      √ only succeeds from a non-zero revoked address to a whitelisted one, and never changes totalSupply (254ms)

  AssetToken AUD-004 regression
    √ rejects a transfer to an unapproved investor without changing balances (41ms)

  AssetToken — Phase 2
    A — deployment and fixed supply
      √ sets the name, symbol, and whole-unit decimals
      √ mints exactly 10,000 whole units to the designated issuer
      √ emits the standard mint Transfer from the zero address
      √ whitelists the issuer and leaves other addresses, including zero, ineligible
      √ grants the issuer the administrator and compliance roles
      √ rejects a zero issuer
      √ rejects a zero supply
      √ exposes no mint or burn entry point, even to the administrator
      √ assigns supply and roles to the issuer instead of a different deploying account
      √ supports construction by a contract without granting that contract authority
    B — whitelist and role administration
      √ approves investors B and C with publicly readable status
      √ emits investor, status, and operator on approval and revocation
      √ rejects unauthorised whitelist update approved=true
      √ rejects a zero investor when approved=true
      √ rejects unauthorised whitelist update approved=false
      √ rejects a zero investor when approved=false
      √ treats repeated status updates as no-ops without duplicate events
      √ revokes a funded investor without confiscation, burning, or Transfer events
      √ delegates compliance and records the actual delegate as operator
      √ prevents unauthorised accounts from granting themselves compliance authority
      √ prevents compliance-only delegates from granting roles
      √ removes compliance authority immediately when the role is revoked
      √ requires compliance authority even for an administrator whose compliance role was revoked
    C — direct transfers and revocation
      √ transfers 100 units to B and emits the standard Transfer event
      √ rejects an unapproved recipient and preserves balances and supply
      √ allows B to transfer 20 units to approved C
      √ rejects a revoked sender even when the recipient is approved
      √ rejects transfers into a revoked holder with a positive balance
      √ rejects transfers when both holder and recipient are ineligible
      √ restores sending and receiving after reapproval
      √ applies the same eligibility requirements to the issuer
    D — approvals and delegated transfers
      √ creates and replaces allowances with standard Approval events
      √ allows an unwhitelisted spender to transfer between approved holders
      √ rolls back balances and allowance for an ineligible delegated recipient
      √ checks the actual holder and rolls back allowance when that holder is revoked
      √ preserves the standard insufficient-allowance error and unchanged state
      √ preserves approvals and approval revocation for ineligible wallets
      √ preserves unlimited allowances across successful transfers
      √ rolls back allowance spending when the delegated holder has insufficient balance
    E — edge cases, contract holders, and instance isolation
      √ rejects a direct transfer exceeding the balance
      √ permits a zero-value transfer between eligible accounts and emits Transfer
      √ enforces both eligibility checks even for zero-value transfers
      √ allows zero-value delegated transfers but still checks holder eligibility
      √ preserves standard zero-recipient errors for direct and delegated transfers
      √ preserves standard errors for a zero spender or a zero delegated owner
      √ supports self-transfers without altering balance or supply
      √ allows an approved smart-contract holder to receive and forward inventory
      √ isolates roles, whitelist, allowances, balances, and supply across assets (46ms)
      √ conserves supply across deterministic transfers, revocation, and failures
    Local demonstration
      √ demonstrates CPT issuance, unapproved D rejection, and final balances 9,900 / 80 / 20

  AUD-012 Sepolia deployment build profile
    √ deploy:sepolia builds with a profile that enables the optimizer for every compiler

  AUD-014 every Hardhat build path
    √ every npm script that compiles through hardhat run, test or compile uses an optimised profile

  AUD-011 frontend lockfile portability
    √ records every optional dependency, so npm ci works on every platform

  Phase 3 audit-1 regressions
    AUD-005 invariant 1 through a revocation
      √ a revoked holder keeps its balance but cannot send, receive or buy until re-approved (45ms)
    AUD-006 issuer admin role cannot be removed
      √ the issuer cannot renounce DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE
      √ a delegated administrator cannot revoke the issuer's DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE but can drop its own
      √ compliance stays revocable, and the issuer can always restore it and recover locked inventory
    AUD-007 forced ETH
      √ selfdestruct really forces ETH past the missing receive function (48ms)
      √ the issuer can recover forced ETH after withdrawing proceeds, leaving the sale empty
      √ sweeping before withdrawal takes only the excess and leaves owed proceeds claimable
      √ only the issuer can sweep, and only when there is excess
    AUD-008 admin cannot be an issuer
      √ authorizeIssuer(admin) reverts and the admin stays unauthorised


